  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of Life-Saving Assistance to Individuals in Libya Affected by the Conflict in Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Heavy clashes between the Rapid Support Forces and the Sudanese Armed Forces that erupted on 15 April 2023 across Sudan has resulted in the displacement of more than 6 million people, with more than 2 million people having crossed to Sudan&#x2019;s neighbouring countries to flee the conflict. Libyan authorities in the East and West are allowing Sudanese to enter the country, authorizing onward movement for those whose health status has been confirmed to be satisfactory, and providing different assistance through various municipal level authorities. However, with an increasing number of Sudanese arriving since the onset of the war in April 2023, the assistance and services provided locally through public facilities are stretched to a maximum and require additional support. In support of government-led and community-led efforts, the United Nations Country Team in Libya launched the Interagency Refugee Response Plan. Within the framework of this plan, IOM is providing life-saving assistance including primary healthcare assistance, mental health and psychosocial support, protection and direct assistance to Sudanese, third country nationals and host communities.</narrative>
  </description>
